2. Compare the results
Interpret your output using these guidelines:
- Baseline: Usually has an \(R^2\) near zero because it predicts the average target value for every listing.
- Decision Tree: Can learn nonlinear relationships and interactions between features. However, it can overfit the training data.
- Random Forest: Combines many decision trees, often improving generalization and reducing overfitting. It is less interpretable and can require more computation.
A higher test \(R^2\) indicates a better fit on unseen data. A negative \(R^2\) means the model performs worse than predicting the test-set mean.
